    "reconstructed": true,
    "shot": "diagram",
    "source": "\n**Frame:** Curt inks connecting lines across the sketch from panel 2 — a web, every agent lane joined to every other. ChatGPT's block returns beside it with the source passage open, one sentence held up against the drawing.\n\n**Screen / system text — CHATGPT:**\n> The record has no line between two runs. Each one writes into the cache and reads what is already there. Draw the web and you have drawn a network nobody found.\n\n**Action:** Curt erases every connecting line. The lanes end up touching the directory tree and nothing else — the geometry pages [001](001.md)–[005](005.md) already use.\n\n**Dialogue — CURT:**\n> That's the first thing I had wrong. And I'd have drawn it a hundred more times.\n\n**Provenance:** `reconstructed` + `documented` — the exchange is invented; the distinction it turns on is documented. `METR` describes isolated workloads communicating through shared writable state, with no direct channel between any two runs, which is why no page in this book joins a pair. The correction runs from the model to the human.\n\n",
    "title": "The corrected shared cache"
  }
}
